ed  space is treated as profane because this  can get messy and complicated very fast  as demonstrated in the Thera me and  argument video it's important to drill  down to the root exchange as many people  pointed out both the therapy and  argument and its game rules sibling  actually argue past the criticism that  they respond to there are in these  exchanges to conversate  going on the first being criticism of  some element or another and the second  being a rejection of the act of  criticizing when someone says it's all  okay because assassin 47 docs points for  killing strippers they're not saying  that a point loss makes the game not  sexist they're rejecting the notion that  the question why is this here even needs  to be asked in the first place this  process of appealing to rules or to an  in-universe explanation is really just a  ritual or a token that's why the theorem  Ian's arguments tend to be flimsy and  mercurial it's not about the criticism  it's about the act of criticizing the  therapy and argument then is more akin  to casting a vote than engaging in a  discussion this resistance to certain  forms of critical analysis isn't limited  to depiction of sex work and other  politically charged topics either it is  broadly applied to any criticism that  breaks the quarantine take for example  the idea of ludonarrative dissonance if  you haven't heard the term before or  have heard it being tossed around with a  snickering grin but don't know what it  means it can be best defined as moments  in a game where the mechanics and rules  and point systems disagree with the  story or encourage behavior counter to  the story's tone and themes the ludo  narrative is in a phrase the story told  by the game's mechanics let's say  assassin 47 starts the game with a long  monologue about how every life is  valuable and killing is a terrible evil  but maybe sometimes it's justified even  then who should have the power to choose  who lives and who dies  who can we trust